<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Playwright" Version="1.59.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Playwright.NUnit" Version="1.59.0" />
    <!-- CI-infra FAULT B fix (automation-test-pr-smoke / issue #131): Microsoft.Playwright
         1.59.0 is a netstandard2.0 library that depends on Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces
         6.0.0 — the legacy POLYFILL for IAsyncDisposable. On .NET 10 IAsyncDisposable is an
         in-box runtime type (System.Private.CoreLib).

         ROOT CAUSE (IL-confirmed): the Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces 6.0.0 package ships TWO
         lib variants — lib/netstandard2.1/ is a pure TYPE-FORWARDER (forwards
         System.IAsyncDisposable to the runtime, harmless) while lib/netstandard2.0/ carries a
         REAL System.IAsyncDisposable typedef. The build's copy-local of the 6.0.0 *compile*
         asset deployed the netstandard2.0 variant (the one with the real typedef) into the
         test output dir, where it SHADOWS the in-box .NET 10 type: Playwright's
         IBrowserContext inherits its IAsyncDisposable.DisposeAsync() slot, the loader binds
         that slot through the polyfill assembly's mismatched layout, and the JIT cannot
         resolve the actual method body — `System.EntryPointNotFoundException : Entry point
         was not found.` (bare message, `at System.IAsyncDisposable.DisposeAsync()` with no
         frames below) on every `await Context.DisposeAsync()` in
         AutomationTest.OneTimeTearDownAsync. CI runs 25879196315 + 25880541230 hit this 11x
         deterministically.

         WHY THE PRIOR ExcludeAssets="runtime" FIX FAILED: it only strips the package's
         *runtime* asset from deps.json — but the 6.0.0 *compile* asset is still copy-local'd
         to output by MSBuild's ResolveAssemblyReference, so the real-typedef DLL kept landing
         in the publish closure (CI run 25880541230's tests-linux-x64 artifact still carried
         it, 24912 bytes — IL-confirmed netstandard2.0 variant, 13 real typedefs).

         FIX: bump the explicit reference to 10.0.7 (the package version matching the CI
         runtime's Microsoft.NETCore.App 10.0.7; verified to exist on nuget.org). NuGet
         "highest version wins" unifies the whole graph to 10.0.7, and for a net10.0 TFM the
         resolved lib asset is lib/netstandard2.1/ — IL-confirmed a pure TYPE-FORWARDER
         (System.IAsyncDisposable forwards to System.Private.CoreLib, 0 real shadowing
         typedefs). The forwarder DLL is still copy-local'd to output, but it is HARMLESS:
         the in-box .NET 10 IAsyncDisposable is the type actually used at runtime.
         Playwright's `Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces, Version=6.0.0.0` assembly reference
         binds against 10.0.0.7 via .NET's default assembly-version roll-forward.
         Locally verified: `dotnet publish -c Release -f net10.0` of this project deploys the
         20744-byte netstandard2.1 forwarder (NOT the 24912-byte netstandard2.0 polyfill). -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces" Version="10.0.7" />
    <!-- Plan 19-01 Open Question 1 verdict = raw-SQLite seed helpers. Version
         matched to the backend's canonical SQLite ADO pin in
         NzbDrone.Common/Mangarr.Common.csproj (System.Data.SQLite 2.0.3) so the
         TestKit.Seed{HistoryFailed,Blocklist,PendingQueueItem}Async helpers open
         <AppData>/mangarr.db with the same provider the backend itself uses. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Data.SQLite" Version="2.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
